            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"1-subword-bpe-hits-a-floor\">1&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;Subword BPE hits a floor</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Ordinary BPE merges <strong>within</strong> a word: bytes &rarr; subwords &rarr; whole word.\nThe best it can ever do is <strong>one token per word</strong>, so <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(X \\to 1.0||)</marimo-tex> &mdash; it never\nmerges across the space between two words.</span>\n<div class=\"language-tsql codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"ss\">&quot;the government of the union.&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">government</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">of</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"ow\">union</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">faithful</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">units</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">frozen</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"o\">][</span><span class=\"n\">government</span><span class=\"o\">][</span><span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"o\">][</span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"o\">][</span><span class=\"n\">union</span><span class=\"o\">][</span><span class=\"n\">.</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tokens</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">best</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">1.0</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Why Indic floors at ~1.5, not 1.0.</strong> A Latin letter is <strong>1 byte</strong>; a Devanagari or\nTelugu character is <strong>3 bytes</strong> in UTF-8. A byte-level tokenizer must first spend merges\njust rebuilding one visible character out of 3 byte-pieces <em>before</em> it can combine\ncharacters into a word. With a shared 10k vocab English grabs <strong>~77%</strong> of the merges\n(see the diagnostic below), so the Indic scripts never finish climbing &mdash; their\nfertility floors around <strong>1.45&ndash;1.51</strong>. No budget shuffling gets pure subword BPE\nbelow ~1.2 for them.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
